= Genetic inheritance =

== Definition ==
'''Genetic inheritance''' is the transmission of genetic material, including DNA sequence variants and genomic organization, among cells or across organismal generations. Its molecular mechanisms depend on the organism, reproductive cycle and transmission pathway. The offspring or daughter cell that inherits material is not necessarily genetically identical to the source, nor is it numerically the same organism.

== Molecular basis ==
DNA stores sequence information in nucleotides. Replication and repair systems copy it, while division and reproductive mechanisms distribute chromosomes or other genetic elements. Mutation can alter sequence; recombination exchanges sequence segments. Phenotype results from genetic variants interacting with regulation and environment, not from a simple sequence-to-trait lookup.

== Eukaryotic reproduction ==
In many sexually reproducing eukaryotes, meiosis yields haploid gametes through chromosome segregation and recombination. Fertilization creates a diploid descendant, combining genetic contributions from parents. Segregation and independent assortment underlie Mendelian expectations for specific inheritance models. Real traits often involve gene interactions, linkage, polygenic effects, environment and non-Mendelian mechanisms.

== Bacterial inheritance ==
Bacteria reproduce through cell division and distribute replicated genetic material to daughter cells, but they can also acquire genetic material horizontally. Transformation, conjugation, transduction and other mechanisms can transfer DNA outside a simple parent–offspring tree. Whether a transferred element persists and spreads depends on function, compatibility, selection and ecological context.

== Beyond DNA sequence ==
Some heritable regulatory states can arise through epigenetic mechanisms. Transmission across generations must be demonstrated rather than inferred from one-generation effects. The extent and molecular basis are species-specific and often more constrained in mammals than in several plant or invertebrate systems. This is distinct from straightforward DNA sequence inheritance.

== Information, computation and evolution ==
DNA can be described as encoding information and gene-regulatory networks can be analyzed computationally. Such analysis can be powerful without equating living cells with ordinary deterministic digital computers. In evolution, mutation, recombination, selection, drift and gene flow play different roles in changing inherited variation.

== Relationship to informational inheritance ==
[[Informational inheritance]] is a broader comparative systems concept; genetic inheritance is a specific biological set of mechanisms with molecular evidence, not merely a metaphor for copied software state.

